+
+The suite prints deprecation warnings: Rails 7.0 warns about `Date#to_s(:db)`
+in fixtures and about `remove_deprecated_time_with_zone_name`. On Ruby 3.2, Rails
+6.1 prints `DidYouMean::SPELL_CHECKERS` deprecations. These are warnings, not
+failures.
+
+## Work left for the next release
+
+1. Add `config.load_defaults 7.0` near the top of the class body in
+   `config/application.rb`, before the Redmine settings. The Redmine settings and
+   the pin block (which comes last) then override the defaults.
+2. Set `config.active_record.belongs_to_required_by_default = false` until the 98
+   lines in `app/models` that mention `belongs_to` have been checked one by one.
+3. Set `config.action_controller.raise_on_open_redirects = false`, or change
+   `redirect_to_referer_or` (`application_controller.rb`) to accept only same-host
+   referers.
+4. Set `config.action_dispatch.return_only_request_media_type_on_content_type = true`.
+5. Replace `to_s(:db)` in `test/fixtures/*.yml` and `test/unit/query_test.rb` (see
+   the table), or keep `disable_to_s_conversion` off.
+6. Decide the remaining behavior items (`smtp_timeout`, origin check,
+   `wrap_parameters_by_default`) and record each decision here.
+7. `test_session_cookie_written_now_is_decryptable_by_the_previous_release_format`
+   fails once the next release writes AES-GCM cookies, which is expected. Keep the
+   guard for this release's writes, and rely on
+   `test_session_cookie_from_rails_7_0_is_readable_on_this_release` for the next
+   release's writes. Do not weaken the guard to make it pass.
+8. Keep the pins. Remove one only after the release that depends on it is no
+   longer a rollback target, and only after checking every reader of that format.
+9. Re-check the API template patch in `10-patches.rb` on every Rails upgrade. It
+   depends on Rails 7.0 internals: `ActionView::Template::Types`,
+   `ActionView::TemplateDetails::Requested`, and `Resolver#find_all`.
+10. Later, not part of the next release: moving sessions to JSON needs string-keyed
+    session data (`session[:auth_source_registration][:login]` and the query session
+    state use symbol keys today). Moving mail links and cookies to SHA256 needs
+    readers for both key digests during a transition.
+11. Autologin cookies (`autologin` in `account_controller.rb`) already set
+    `same_site: :lax`. With `cookies_same_site_protection` from the 6.1 defaults,
+    other cookies would get `SameSite=Lax`. Mail links are top-level GET
+    navigations, which Lax still sends cookies for. Check this in the next release.
+
+## Checklist for each release
+
+1. Run the full suite with `Gemfile`, and with `Gemfile.next` if the release
+   touches Rails. Run `bin/rails test test/unit/rollback_compatibility_test.rb`
+   with both.
+2. Diff `config/application.rb` against this file. Every pin must still be there,
+   or its removal must be recorded here with the reason.
+3. Do the rollback drill in the appendix before deploying: write with the new
+   release, read with the previous one, and the reverse.
+4. Record the rollback result in this file.
+
+## Appendix: rollback drill
+
+Run the drill on a host with the production secret, with `RAILS_ENV=production`.
+It writes only to a scratch directory and does not touch the database. Both
+releases must have the same secret.
+
+Write with the release being deployed (`DRILL_DIR` is a scratch directory):
+
+    mkdir -p /tmp/drill
+    DRILL_DIR=/tmp/drill bin/rails runner -e production drill_write.rb
+
+Read with the release being rolled back to, and with the release being deployed:
+
+    DRILL_DIR=/tmp/drill bin/rails runner -e production drill_read.rb
+
+`drill_write.rb`:
+
+```ruby
+# Writes a session cookie, a mail-link token and a file-cache entry with the release that runs this script.
+dir = ENV.fetch('DRILL_DIR')
+token = Rails.application.message_verifier(:mail_links).generate(
+  {'user_id' => 1, 'issue_id' => 1}, purpose: :unwatch, expires_in: 30.days)
+request = ActionDispatch::Request.new(Rails.application.env_config.dup)
+request.cookie_jar.signed_or_encrypted['_redmine_session'] = {value: {'user_id' => 1, 'tk' => 'drill'}, path: '/'}
+headers = {}
+request.cookie_jar.write(headers)
+ActiveSupport::Cache::FileStore.new(File.join(dir, 'cache')).write('drill/formatted_text', {'html' => '<p>drill</p>'})
+File.write(File.join(dir, 'artifacts.txt'),
+  "MAIL_TOKEN=#{token}\nSESSION_COOKIE=#{Array(headers['Set-Cookie']).first.split(';').first}\n")
+puts "written to #{dir}"
+```
+
+`drill_read.rb`:
+
+```ruby
+# Reads what another release wrote. Run it with the release you are rolling back to.
+dir = ENV.fetch('DRILL_DIR')
+values = File.readlines(File.join(dir, 'artifacts.txt')).map { |line| line.chomp.split('=', 2) }.to_h
+puts "mail link: #{Mailer.verify_mail_link(values['MAIL_TOKEN'], :unwatch).inspect}"
+request = ActionDispatch::Request.new(Rails.application.env_config.merge('HTTP_COOKIE' => values['SESSION_COOKIE']))
+puts "session:   #{request.cookie_jar.signed_or_encrypted['_redmine_session'].inspect}"
+puts "cache:     #{ActiveSupport::Cache::FileStore.new(File.join(dir, 'cache')).read('drill/formatted_text').inspect}"
+```
+
+Every line of the read output must be a value, not `nil` and not an error. A
+`nil` session means users would be signed out. A `nil` mail link or an error means
+links in sent mail would break.
